insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 1, 'In the open hole, the mud pressure has to sit between which two pressures at every depth?', '["The pore pressure below it and the fracture pressure above it","The hydrostatic pressure below it and the overburden stress above it","The pore pressure below it and the overburden stress above it","The hydrostatic pressure below it and the fracture pressure above it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mud pressure below the pore pressure lets formation fluid into the well as a kick, and mud pressure above the fracture pressure pushes mud into the rock as lost circulation. Hydrostatic and overburden bound what the well can hold, and they are not the walls the mud weight is managed against.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 2, 'Which failure is caused by carrying too much mud weight against a permeable bed?', '["Gas expanding as it rises in the wellbore","Differential sticking","A kick","A blowout"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A large excess of mud pressure over pore pressure presses the drill string into the filter cake on the borehole wall and holds it there. The other three are consequences of underbalance, which is the opposite error.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 3, 'The golden well is sampled every 10 m. What are its total depth and its water depth?', '["4000 m below sea level in 100 m of water","4000 m below mudline in 1000 m of water","4000 m below mudline in 100 m of water","4100 m below mudline in 100 m of water"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fixture has 401 samples from 0 to 4000 m below mudline at a 10 m interval, in 100 m of water. Depths in this course are quoted below mudline unless stated otherwise.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 4, 'Which statement about depth references in the golden well is correct?', '["4000 m below mudline is 4000 m below sea level, because the mudline is the datum for both","4000 m below mudline is 4100 m below sea level, and 4100 m is the height to use in the sediment term of the hydrostatic column","4000 m below sea level is 4100 m below mudline, because the water depth is subtracted","4000 m below mudline is 4100 m below sea level, because the water depth of 100 m is added"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Depths in this course are quoted below mudline, and the water depth is added to reach a depth below sea level. Putting 4100 m into the sediment term counts the water twice, once as seawater and once as pore fluid.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 5, 'At the mudline in the golden well, the hydrostatic pressure and the overburden stress are equal. What is that shared value and why are they equal there?', '["1.005182 MPa, because above the mudline both quantities are the same 100 m of seawater","0 MPa, because no sediment has accumulated at the mudline","1.005182 MPa, because the bulk density log begins at the mudline","41.408580 MPa, because both curves are referenced to sea level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no rock above the seabed, so both quantities consist of the same seawater column and there is nothing to separate them. A hydrostatic curve reading zero at the mudline has left the water column out.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 6, 'Which of the three pressures is estimated rather than computed from geometry and density?', '["The seawater part of the hydrostatic column","Pore pressure","Hydrostatic pressure","Overburden stress"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hydrostatic comes from a fluid density, gravity and a height, and overburden comes from integrating a bulk density log, so both are calculations. Pore pressure has to be estimated, usually from the departure of a log from a compaction trend.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 7, 'In any sane well, what is the correct ordering of the three pressures at a given depth?', '["Overburden is less than or equal to pore pressure, which is less than or equal to hydrostatic","Hydrostatic is less than or equal to overburden, which is less than or equal to pore pressure","Hydrostatic is less than or equal to pore pressure, which is less than or equal to overburden","Pore pressure is less than or equal to hydrostatic, which is less than or equal to overburden"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A pore pressure at the overburden leaves the grain contacts carrying nothing and the rock parts, so a computed value above the overburden curve indicates an arithmetic error. A value below hydrostatic requires active drainage and is a flagged special case.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 8, 'In the golden well at 4000 m below mudline, what are the overburden stress and the hydrostatic pressure?', '["Overburden 41.408579625 MPa and hydrostatic 91.12306695073282 MPa","Overburden 91.12306695073282 MPa and hydrostatic 40.403398 MPa","Overburden 54.952589 MPa and hydrostatic 26.257305 MPa","Overburden 91.12306695073282 MPa and hydrostatic 41.408579625 MPa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The overburden at total depth is more than twice the hydrostatic value, which is the room a pore pressure has to move in. A hydrostatic value of 40.403398 MPa is the classic result of omitting the seawater column, and the pair 54.952589 and 26.257305 MPa belongs at 2500 m below mudline.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 9, 'What form does Gardner''s relation take in this course?', '["rho = 310 x v^0.25, with v in m/s and rho in kg/m3","rho = 0.25 x v^310, with v in m/s and rho in kg/m3","rho = 310 x v, with v in m/s and rho in kg/m3","v = 310 x rho^0.25, with v in m/s and rho in kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Density is predicted from velocity with an exponent of one quarter, so at 1600 m/s the relation returns 1960.612149304395 kg/m3. The exponent makes density a slow function of velocity.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 10, 'What density does Gardner''s relation return at a velocity of 1600 m/s?', '["2294.256693926084 kg/m3","1960.612149304395 kg/m3","2073.094945426908 kg/m3","2192.0310216782973 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is one of the six graded values in the capstone, quoted to a tolerance of 0.5 kg/m3. The other three options are the Gardner densities at 2000, 2500 and 3000 m/s.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 11, 'Why is Gardner''s relation needed in the overburden workflow?', '["It replaces the seawater part of the hydrostatic column in a marine well","It fits the compaction trend to the shale picks","A density log rarely reaches the seabed, so density has to be predicted from velocity over the shallow section or the integration loses the weight above the top of the log","It converts transit time directly into pore pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The overburden is an integral of density from the top of the section down, so a missing shallow interval removes real weight from every value below it. Gardner fills that gap from a velocity.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 12, 'Using the golden well''s own trend parameters of 656 us/m at the mudline, a matrix value of 220 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.0006 per m, what is the trend transit time at 2500 m below mudline?', '["294.722796 us/m","292.070315 us/m","297.76677602422825 us/m","317.2847498247154 us/m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a graded capstone value with a tolerance of 0.5 us/m, and the log reads the same value at that depth. The value 294.722796 us/m is the fitted trend at 2500 m, while 292.070315 and 297.76677602422825 us/m are the trend and the log at 3000 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 13, 'A least squares fit through the twelve shale picks returns a mudline transit time of 650.0000000000014 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.7000000000000015 per km, while the well header states 656 us/m and 0.6 per km. What is the right reading of that disagreement?', '["The fit reports what the picks it was given actually say, so fitted parameters have to be checked for physical sense before a prognosis is built on them","The fit is faulty and should be rerun with the header values imposed on it","The header is wrong and should be overwritten with the fitted values without further checks","The two disagree because gravity was rounded during the fit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fitted trend describes the data it was handed, and these picks were drawn on a different trend from the one written in the header. Hand a fit the wrong points and it returns a confident number describing the wrong points.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 14, 'Where does the overpressure ramp begin in the golden well, and what is the evidence for it at 3000 m below mudline?', '["At 4000 m below mudline, where the log reads 270.92263512383806 us/m","At 2500 m below mudline, and at 3000 m the log reads 297.76677602422825 us/m against a trend value of 292.070315 us/m","At 2500 m below mudline, and at 3000 m the log reads 292.070315 us/m against a trend value of 297.76677602422825 us/m","At the mudline, and the log agrees with the trend at every depth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The log sits exactly on the trend at 2500 m and is slower than the trend below it, which is the undercompaction signature the ramp encoded. A transit time above the trend means a more porous and slower rock than the burial depth would suggest.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-pressure-in-the-subsurface', 15, 'How does fracture pressure fit into this tier?', '["It is another name for the overburden stress","It is the hydrostatic pressure referenced to sea level","It is the fourth curve and is added at the Professional tier, because it cannot be computed until a pore pressure estimate exists","It is built in module 3 by integrating the bulk density log"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fracture pressure depends on the pore pressure and on the horizontal stress the rock can support, so it needs a pore pressure curve to stand on. Together with pore pressure it forms the two walls of the mud weight window.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 1, 'Which situation breaks the assumption that a formation sits at the hydrostatic pressure?', '["The pore is sealed inside an impermeable shale with no continuous fluid path to the sea","The formation water is slightly more saline than seawater","The well stands in 100 m of water","The bulk density log is missing over the shallow section"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hydrostatic requires a connected column, standing to a free surface, at rest. Salinity changes the fluid density that goes into the calculation, water depth is part of the column, and a missing density log affects the overburden rather than the hydrostatic baseline.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 2, 'What is hydrostatic pressure?', '["The pressure at which a formation takes fluid from the wellbore","The pressure at the base of a connected column of fluid standing to the free surface and supporting only its own weight","The pressure recorded by a wireline pressure tool in a permeable bed","The total weight per unit area of the rock and fluid above the point of interest"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rock contributes nothing to it, since the grain frame carries its own weight through grain contacts. The third option describes the overburden and the fourth describes fracture pressure.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 3, 'Why is hydrostatic pressure used as the baseline for pore pressure work?', '["It is equal to the overburden stress at all depths below the mudline","It is the highest pressure the pore fluid can reach","It is computed from water depth, fluid density, gravity and depth, so it is reproducible and independent of the geology between two wells","It is measured in every well before drilling begins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every pore pressure statement is a statement about a departure from hydrostatic, so the reference has to be something two people will agree on. Treating hydrostatic as a measurement rather than as a computed baseline is a common conceptual error.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 4, 'In the golden well, what is the seawater part of the hydrostatic column, computed as 1025 x 9.80665 x 100?', '["1005181.625 MPa","40403398 Pa","41408579.625 Pa","1005181.625 Pa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The seawater term uses the seawater density of 1025 kg/m3 and the water depth of 100 m. The value 40403398 Pa is the sediment term and 41408579.625 Pa is the total at total depth, while the megapascal version is out by a factor of a million.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 5, 'In the golden well, what is the sediment part of the hydrostatic column at total depth, computed as 1030 x 9.80665 x 4000?', '["40403398 Pa","40.403398 Pa","1005181.625 Pa","41408579.625 Pa"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sediment term uses the pore fluid density of 1030 kg/m3 and the depth below mudline of 4000 m. Quoting it as 40.403398 Pa confuses pascals with megapascals, and the other two values are the seawater term and the total.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 6, 'What is the hydrostatic pressure at 4000 m below mudline in the golden well?', '["41408579.625 MPa","41408579.625 Pa, which is 41.408579625 MPa","40403398 Pa, which is 40.403398 MPa","41.408579625 Pa"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two parts of the column, 1005181.625 Pa of seawater and 40403398 Pa of pore fluid, add to the graded value. The 40.403398 MPa option is the classic result of leaving the seawater column out, and the other two carry the wrong unit.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 7, 'A hydrostatic curve is built with the seawater term omitted. By how much is it in error in the golden well, and what does that do to pore pressure estimates built on it?', '["It is low by an amount that grows with depth, which makes pore pressure estimates too low","It is low by 40403398 Pa at total depth, which has no effect on pore pressure estimates","It is low by 1005181.625 Pa at every depth, which makes pore pressure estimates too high","It is low by 1005181.625 Pa at every depth, which makes pore pressure estimates too low"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The seawater term is the same at every depth, so the whole baseline is shifted down by a constant and never announces itself as a trend. A baseline that is too low makes every departure from it look larger than it is.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 8, 'What is the hydrostatic pressure at 2000 m below mudline in the golden well?', '["26.257305 MPa","11.106031 MPa","41.408580 MPa","21.206881 MPa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two part sum at that depth gives 21.206881 MPa. The other options are the hydrostatic values at 2500 m, 1000 m and 4000 m below mudline, which is why a pressure quoted without its depth is not an answer.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 9, 'Which statement about a pressure and a gradient is correct?', '["A pressure is a value at a depth and has to be quoted with that depth and its datum, while a gradient is a rate of change of pressure with depth over an interval","A gradient has to be quoted with a depth, while a pressure applies everywhere in the well","A pressure and a gradient are the same quantity in different units, so either can be quoted on its own","A pressure of 41.408579625 MPa is a complete answer on its own"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pressures carry units of Pa or MPa and mean nothing without a depth and a datum, while gradients carry units such as Pa/m or kPa/m and describe an interval. The equivalent mud weight is a third quantity, an average gradient from a datum expressed as a density.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 10, 'Which value of gravity does this engine use, and why is it stated to five decimal places?', '["9.80665 m/s2, because local gravity was measured at the well site","9.80665 m/s2, because it multiplies the whole 4222500 kg/m2 column, so an error in it passes straight into the answer","9.81 m/s2, because the extra digits make no measurable difference at 4000 m","9.8 m/s2, because pressure work is quoted to two significant figures"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The value is a defined standard rather than a site measurement, and the whole hydrostatic answer is proportional to it. The column of 1025 x 100 plus 1030 x 4000 is 4222500 kg/m2, so any error in gravity is multiplied by that figure.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 11, 'The hydrostatic pressure at 4000 m below mudline is computed with g = 9.81 m/s2 instead of 9.80665 m/s2. What is the result and the error?', '["41.380500000 MPa, an error of -0.028079625 MPa","41.422725000 MPa, an error of -0.028079625 MPa","41.422725000 MPa, an error of +0.014145375 MPa","41.408579625 MPa, with no error at all"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rounded gravity value returns a number that has the right magnitude, the right unit and a plausible spread of digits, and it is wrong by 14 kilopascals. The value 41.380500000 MPa and its error belong to g = 9.8 m/s2.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 12, 'The capstone tolerance on the hydrostatic pressure at total depth is 0.01 MPa. Which of the rounded gravity values 9.8 m/s2 and 9.81 m/s2 passes it?', '["Both, since each is within 0.01 MPa of the graded value","Only 9.81 m/s2, since its error of +0.014145375 MPa is inside the tolerance","Only 9.8 m/s2, since rounding gravity down is the conservative choice","Neither, since 9.8 m/s2 is out by -0.028079625 MPa and 9.81 m/s2 is out by +0.014145375 MPa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both errors are larger than the 0.01 MPa tolerance, so both rounded constants are marked wrong. They also fall on opposite sides of the graded value, so rounding gravity does not even give a consistent bias.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 13, 'How is equivalent mud weight defined in this course?', '["EMW = P divided by g times the sum of the depth below mudline and the water depth, referenced to sea level","EMW = P divided by g times the depth below mudline, referenced to the mudline","EMW = P multiplied by g times the sum of the depth below mudline and the water depth","EMW = P divided by g times the water depth"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mud column stands from sea level in this treatment, so its height is the water depth plus the depth below mudline. A value quoted against a different datum is a different number.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 14, 'The equivalent mud weight of the hydrostatic column at total depth is 1029.878049 kg/m3 while the pore fluid density is 1030 kg/m3. Why is it slightly lower?', '["The equivalent mud weight is referenced to the mudline rather than to sea level","The top 100 m of the column is seawater at 1025 kg/m3, which pulls the average density of the whole column just below the pore fluid value","Gravity was rounded when the equivalent mud weight was computed","The pore fluid density falls with depth in the engine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The column averaged from sea level is 100 m of the lighter seawater on top of 4000 m of pore fluid. A result of exactly 1030 kg/m3 would mean the seawater had been left out of both the pressure and the height.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-hydrostatic-column', 15, 'Which pair of equivalent mud weights brackets every pressure the golden well can hold at total depth, and where does each come from?', '["1025 kg/m3 from seawater and 1030 kg/m3 from the pore fluid","2266.333384 kg/m3 from the hydrostatic curve and 1029.878049 kg/m3 from the overburden curve","1029.878049 kg/m3 from the hydrostatic curve and 2266.333384 kg/m3 from the overburden curve","1030 kg/m3 from the pore fluid density and 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 from the bulk density log"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The lower wall is the hydrostatic column and the upper wall is the total weight of the overlying section, both expressed as densities at total depth. Pore pressure sits inside that bracket and the mud weight sits above the pore pressure and below the fracture pressure.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 1, 'What does the overburden stress at a given depth represent?', '["The weight of everything above that depth, seawater and bulk rock together, expressed as a stress","The weight of the pore fluid alone, from sea level down to that depth","The weight of the rock grains alone, with the pore fluid excluded","The part of the load carried by grain to grain contact at that depth"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Overburden is the total vertical stress, so it counts the whole column including the fluid in the pore space, which is what the bulk density log reads. Option 2 describes the hydrostatic and option 4 describes effective stress.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 2, 'At the mudline of the golden well the hydrostatic and the overburden are both 1.005182 MPa. Why are they equal there?', '["Because the density log begins at the mudline, so the overburden integration starts from zero","Because above the mudline both quantities are the same 100 m column of seawater at 1025 kg/m3","Because pore fluid at 1030 kg/m3 and seawater at 1025 kg/m3 are close enough to be treated as equal","Because the two curves are parallel, so they share their shallowest value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is nothing above the mudline except seawater, so neither quantity has accumulated anything to tell them apart. Option 1 is the classic error of starting the overburden at zero, which drops the water column entirely, and option 4 is the equally common assumption that the two curves are parallel.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 3, 'Which arithmetic gives the seawater part of this well''s column?', '["1030 x 9.80665 x 4000 = 40403398 Pa","1025 x 100 + 1030 x 4000 = 4222500 kg/m2","1025 x 9.80665 x 100 = 1005181.625 Pa","1030 x 9.80665 x 100"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The seawater part uses the seawater density of 1025 kg/m3 over the 100 m water depth, and 1005181.625 Pa is 1.005182 MPa at the mudline. Option 1 is the sediment part, option 2 is the column mass per unit area before gravity is applied, and option 4 uses the pore fluid density for the water column.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 4, 'Vertical effective stress at a depth is:', '["the pore pressure less the overburden","the overburden plus the pore pressure","the part of the load carried by the pore fluid","the overburden less the pore pressure, which is the part of the load carried by grain to grain contact"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Terzaghi''s relation is sigma = S - P, and it is the reason overburden matters, because the grains compact under the effective stress rather than under the total stress or the pore pressure.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 5, 'If the pore pressure at some depth rose all the way to the overburden, what would follow?', '["Effective stress would reach zero, so the grain framework would carry none of the load and the formation would part","Effective stress would reach its maximum value for that depth","The hydrostatic and the overburden curves would cross","The bulk density at that depth would fall to the pore fluid density"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Zero effective stress is the limiting case of sigma = S - P, which is why the overburden is the hard upper bound on pore pressure. Real rock fails earlier, at the fracture pressure, which sits below the overburden.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 6, 'At TD in the golden well the overburden and the hydrostatic are:', '["41.408580 MPa and 91.123067 MPa respectively","91.123067 MPa and 41.408580 MPa respectively","both 1.005182 MPa, as they are at the mudline","91.123067 MPa and 66.831143 MPa respectively"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The graded values at TD are 91.12306695073282 MPa for the overburden and 41.408579625 MPa for the hydrostatic, so the overburden is more than twice the hydrostatic. Option 4 quotes 66.831143 MPa, which is the overburden at 3000 m rather than any hydrostatic value.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 7, 'Why can the overburden of this well not be obtained by applying a single gradient from sea level?', '["Because gravity varies with depth","Because the sonic log is sampled every 10 m","Because the water column is far lighter than rock, and bulk density itself rises from 1900 kg/m3 at the mudline to 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 at TD","Because the pore fluid density of 1030 kg/m3 is not constant"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A column whose density changes by about a third over its length cannot be represented by one gradient, and the 100 m of seawater on top makes the shallow section lighter still. The pore fluid density is held fixed at 1030 kg/m3 in this well, so option 4 is not the reason.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 8, 'A 200 m interval of the density log has to be discarded for bad hole. What does that do to the overburden if it is left unfilled?', '["Only the discarded interval is affected, values above and below it are unchanged","The overburden above the gap is affected but the values below it recover","The overburden is unaffected, because trapezoidal integration bridges gaps automatically","Every overburden value below the gap is shifted, because the integration carries a running sum down from the mudline"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The overburden at any depth already contains every slice above it, so a gap is not a local problem. This is why missing density has to be filled, usually with Gardner, before the integration is run.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 9, 'Which is the metric form of Gardner''s transform as this course uses it?', '["rho = 310 x v^0.25, with velocity in m/s and density in kg/m3","rho = 0.23 x v^0.25, with velocity in m/s and density in kg/m3","rho = 310 x v^4, with velocity in m/s and density in kg/m3","rho = 310 x v^0.25, with velocity in ft/s and density in g/cm3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The coefficient 310 pairs with metres per second and kilograms per cubic metre. The 0.23 coefficient belongs with feet per second and g/cm3, and mixing the two conventions is a large unit error that no downstream check will forgive.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 10, 'Gardner''s transform at a velocity of 1600 m/s returns:', '["2073.094945426908 kg/m3","1960.612149304395 kg/m3","2294.256693926084 kg/m3","1900 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is one of the six graded capstone values, with a tolerance of 0.5 kg/m3. Option 1 is the Gardner density at 2000 m/s and option 3 is the value at 3000 m/s, both from reading the wrong row.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 11, 'What kind of relation is Gardner''s transform?', '["A physical law relating density and velocity exactly","An exact inversion of the sonic tool response","An empirical fit through measurements from many lithologies, good for ordinary clastics and poor for salt, coal and gas bearing sands","A basin specific relation that has to be refitted for every well before it can be used"]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no physics requiring density to be the fourth root of velocity. Treating the transform as exact is a classic error, and it is worst in the lithologies the fit was never drawn through.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 12, 'Gardner densities at 1600 m/s and at 3000 m/s are 1960.612149304395 kg/m3 and 2294.256693926084 kg/m3. What does that comparison tell you?', '["The transform is highly sensitive to velocity, so small velocity errors produce large density errors","Density nearly doubles as velocity nearly doubles, so the two are close to proportional","Density falls with velocity, which is why transit time falls with depth","A near doubling of velocity moves the density very little, so the transform returns a plausible density for almost any input and will not warn you about a bad one"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exponent of 0.25 makes the relation flat, which is forgiving of moderate velocity errors and unable to resolve fine density detail. The check on a Gardner density has to come from you, by asking whether it is sensible for the lithology and depth.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 13, 'Below the mudline the hydrostatic of this well is a straight line while the overburden bends. Why?', '["The hydrostatic accumulates a fixed 1030 kg/m3 pore fluid, while the overburden accumulates bulk density that rises as the sediment compacts","The overburden bends because gravity changes with depth","Both are straight, and the two curves are parallel below the mudline","The hydrostatic bends and the overburden is the straight one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gap between the two widens faster than depth alone would suggest, and that widening gap is the room available for overpressure. Assuming the two curves are parallel is a classic error, and it hides exactly that behaviour.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 14, 'At TD the two frame curves of this well convert to equivalent mud weights of:', '["1030 kg/m3 and 1900 kg/m3","1029.878049 kg/m3 and 2266.333384 kg/m3","1025 kg/m3 and 2505.265301734371 kg/m3","1029.878049 kg/m3 and 2505.265301734371 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hydrostatic converts to 1029.878049 kg/m3, just under the 1030 kg/m3 pore fluid density because the seawater part of the column is slightly lighter, and the overburden converts to 2266.333384 kg/m3. The value 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 in options 3 and 4 is the bulk density of the rock at TD, which is not a mud weight.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-overburden', 15, 'Which value of gravity reproduces the graded hydrostatic pressure at TD?', '["9.8 m/s2, which gives 41.380500000 MPa","9.81 m/s2, which gives 41.422725000 MPa","9.80665 m/s2, which gives 41.408579625 MPa","Any of the three, since the capstone tolerance on that field is 0.01 MPa"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine uses 9.80665 m/s2 for every pressure in this course. Rounding to 9.81 puts the answer out by 0.014145375 MPa and rounding to 9.8 puts it out by 0.028079625 MPa, so both fail the 0.01 MPa tolerance.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 1, 'Why is the normal compaction trend fitted to shale rather than to sand?', '["Because shale velocity is unaffected by the fluid in its pores","Because sands are too rare in a clastic section to define a trend over the depth range","Because the sonic tool cannot read sands reliably","Because shale has very low permeability, so it retains the compaction record, and its porosity tracks effective stress in a repeatable way across a basin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A sand drains, so it keeps no record of retained pressure, and its velocity depends on cement, sorting and pore fluid, so a gas charge alone can imitate a large overpressure. Fitting a shale relation to sand points is a classic way to produce a confident and wrong trend.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 2, 'Which is the form of the normal compaction trend used in this course?', '["dt(z) = dt_ml + (dt_ma - dt_ml) x exp(-c z)","dt(z) = dt_ma + (dt_ml - dt_ma) x exp(c z)","dt(z) = dt_ma + (dt_ml - dt_ma) x exp(-c z)","dt(z) = dt_ma + (dt_ml - dt_ma) x z"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The curve starts at the mudline transit time and decays toward the matrix transit time as depth increases, which requires the negative exponent and the bracket taken in that order. Option 2 grows without limit with depth and option 4 is linear rather than exponential.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 3, 'On the golden well''s own trend parameters, what is the normal compaction transit time at 2500 m below the mudline?', '["294.722796 us/m","317.2847498247154 us/m","351.320676 us/m","259.553028 us/m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is 220 + (656 - 220) x exp(-0.0006 x 2500), and it is one of the six graded capstone values with a tolerance of 0.5 us/m. Option 1 is the fitted trend at the same depth, which is the classic mix up between the header trend and the fitted trend, and option 3 is the well trend at 2000 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 4, 'What do the three parameters of the trend equation mean?', '["dt_ma is the floor the curve approaches, dt_ml is the value at the seabed, and c sets how fast the curve falls between them","dt_ma is the value at the seabed and dt_ml is the floor the curve approaches","c sets the floor of the curve and dt_ma sets the rate of decay","All three are fitted from the shale picks in the standard workflow"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The matrix value is the transit time with no porosity left, 220 us/m here, and the mudline value is the water rich sediment at the seabed, 656 us/m here. Option 4 is wrong for this workflow because the matrix value is fixed from lithology, since fitting all three at once is ill conditioned.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 5, 'The fitted compaction constant for this well is 0.7000000000000015 per km. Which statement is correct?', '["It is the same number as the well header value of 0.0006 per m","Expressed in per metre it would still read 0.7000000000000015","Per km and per m can be used interchangeably for this parameter","The engine carries it in per metre, where it is 0.0007 per m, while the header value of 0.0006 per m is 0.6 per km"]'::jsonb, 3, 'There is a factor of a thousand between the two conventions, and quoting the constant in the wrong unit is a classic error. Written in the same unit the two values are different rates, which is the whole reason the fitted trend and the header trend are different curves.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 6, 'At 3000 m below the mudline the log reads 297.76677602422825 us/m while the well trend reads 292.070315 us/m. What does that say about the shale there?', '["It is more compacted than a normal trend shale at that depth","The sonic tool is reading a sand rather than a shale","It has retained porosity, so it has carried less effective stress than a normally compacted shale, which means the pore pressure is above hydrostatic","The trend must have been fitted to the wrong picks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A transit time above the trend means more porosity than the depth would require, and by sigma = S - P a low effective stress under a fixed overburden means a high pore pressure. This departure is the undercompaction signature, and this well keeps it from 2500 m down to TD.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 7, 'What is special about 2500 m below the mudline in the golden well?', '["It is the depth at which the fitted trend and the well trend cross","It is the top of the overpressure ramp, and the last depth at which the log sits on the well trend, both reading 317.2847498247154 us/m","It is the depth at which the density log ends","It is the deepest of the twelve shale picks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Above that depth the well is normally pressured and the log follows the trend, and below it the log stays above the trend. The deepest of the twelve picks is at 3500 m, so option 4 is wrong.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 8, 'Why is the trend fitted to picked points rather than to every sample of the sonic log?', '["Because a sonic log runs through mixed lithology, and the compaction relation being fitted applies to shale, so fitting the whole log fits a rock that does not exist","Because a least squares fit cannot take more than twelve points","Because the sonic log is sampled too finely for the fit to converge","Because only twelve depths in the well have a valid transit time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mixture of sands, silts, shales and stringers is not evenly distributed with depth, so it tilts the fitted curve instead of averaging out. Washouts, cycle skips and log splices are in the whole log too, and none of them are compaction.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 9, 'What is the depth coverage of the twelve shale picks this capstone fits?', '["Every 100 m from the mudline down to 1100 m","Every 300 m from 2500 m down to 4000 m","Twelve picks clustered between 2600 m and 3500 m","Every 300 m from 200 m down to 3500 m below the mudline"]'::jsonb, 3, 'That spread covers most of the logged interval, which constrains both the steep shallow part of the exponential and the nearly flat deep part. Picks clustered over a few hundred metres, as in options 2 and 3, constrain neither the starting value nor the decay rate.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 10, 'A deep pick is taken in a fast sand instead of a shale. What does that single pick do to the fit?', '["Nothing, because least squares averages it out across the other eleven picks","It flattens the fitted curve and inflates the apparent departure of the log from the trend","It steepens the fitted curve and lowers the trend below it, which shrinks the apparent departure and under predicts pore pressure","It raises the fitted matrix transit time above 220 us/m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sands at depth are usually faster than shales, so the pick sits low and pulls the curve down with it, and under predicting pore pressure is the error that gets wells into trouble. Option 2 describes what a washed out pick does, in the opposite direction, and option 4 is impossible here because the matrix value is fixed rather than fitted.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 11, 'Why must every shale pick have a transit time greater than the matrix value of 220 us/m?', '["Because the sonic tool cannot record values below 220 us/m","Because the fit works on the logarithm of the pick less the matrix value, which is undefined for a pick at or below 220 us/m","Because a pick below 220 us/m would have to be in an overpressured shale","Because the capstone tolerance on a transit time is 0.5 us/m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taking logarithms is what turns the exponential into a straight line in depth and gives the fit a closed form solution. The engine throws with the index of the offending pick rather than returning a quiet NaN, and such a pick means either a bad pick or a bad matrix value.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 12, 'The least squares fit through the twelve picks, with the matrix transit time fixed at 220 us/m, returns:', '["a mudline transit time of 650.0000000000014 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.7000000000000015 per km","a mudline transit time of 656 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.0006 per m","a mudline transit time of 650.0000000000014 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.0006 per m","a mudline transit time of 593.8240412214866 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.7000000000000015 per km"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both fitted values are graded, the first with a tolerance of 0.5 us/m and the second with a tolerance of 0.005 per km. Option 2 is the well header, which is the classic error of reporting the label as the fit, and 593.8240412214866 us/m in option 4 is the shallowest pick, at 200 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 13, 'What does the fitted trend give at 2500 m below the mudline?', '["317.284750 us/m","326.036694 us/m","289.6710729015687 us/m","294.722796 us/m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is the trend equation evaluated with the fitted parameters, a mudline transit time of 650.0000000000014 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.7000000000000015 per km, rather than with the header ones. Option 1 is the well header trend at that depth, which is the mix up to watch for, option 2 is the fitted trend at 2000 m, and option 3 is the shale pick at 2600 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 14, 'The fit reproduces its input picks to fourteen digits and leaves residuals at machine precision. What does that tell you?', '["That the fitted trend is the right trend for this well","That the picks were taken with unusual care on the log display","That the twelve picks are exactly consistent with one exponential, which is what picks drawn from a clean curve look like, and it says nothing about whether that exponential describes this well","That the matrix transit time of 220 us/m was fitted correctly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Picks taken by hand off a real sonic log scatter and leave residuals of a few us/m, and those residuals carry information about the picks and the form of the trend. Assuming that a fit matching to fourteen digits must be describing the right rock is a classic error, and the matrix value in option 4 was fixed rather than fitted.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-compaction-trend', 15, 'The fit says 650.0000000000014 us/m and 0.7000000000000015 per km. The well header says 656 us/m and 0.0006 per m. Which account is correct?', '["The fit is wrong, because it disagrees with the parameters recorded for the well","Neither is wrong. The picks were drawn on a different trend from the one in the header, and the fit reports what the picks actually say","The header is wrong, because a least squares fit with zero residuals cannot be beaten","Both describe the same curve, expressed in different units"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A fitted trend describes the data you gave it, so the fit answered correctly about its twelve picks while the header remains correct about the model the log was built on. Option 4 is the unit confusion, since 0.7000000000000015 per km and 0.0006 per m are different rates and the two curves differ at every depth below the mudline.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 1, 'At the mudline the hydrostatic pressure and the overburden stress on the golden well both read 1.005182 MPa. Why are they the same value there?', '["The pore fluid and the sediment happen to have the same density at the seabed.","The overburden integration is set to start at the first sediment sample rather than at the mudline.","Above the mudline there is nothing but seawater, so the fluid column and the total weight overhead are the same 100 m of 1025 kg/m3 water.","It is an artefact of the 10 m sample spacing and would disappear on a finer log."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both curves are born at the same point because the only thing above the mudline is the 100 m seawater column at 1025 kg/m3. They separate only once sediment starts contributing to the overburden.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 2, 'Which of the two pressure curves on this well plots as a straight line, and why?', '["The hydrostatic, because it uses a single constant pore fluid density of 1030 kg/m3 all the way down, so equal steps in depth add equal steps in pressure.","The overburden, because the sediment density is treated as constant below the mudline.","Both, because pressure always increases linearly with depth in a normally pressured well.","Neither, because the seawater column bends both of them near the mudline."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The hydrostatic depends on one constant fluid density and no rock property, which is what makes it a straight reference line. The overburden bends because the density it integrates grows from 1900 kg/m3 at the mudline to 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 at 4000 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 3, 'The overburden goes from 1.005182 MPa at the mudline to 21.100398 MPa at 1000 m, and from 66.831143 MPa at 3000 m to 91.123067 MPa at 4000 m. Why does the deeper thousand metres add more?', '["The pore fluid density rises with depth, so the fluid contributes more of the load deeper down.","The seawater column contributes a larger share of the overburden as depth increases.","The sonic log is sampled more densely below 3000 m, so more samples enter the integration.","Sediment density rises with depth, from 1900 kg/m3 at the mudline to 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 at 4000 m, so each deeper metre presses down harder than the metre above it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A bending overburden is a compaction signature. If the curve plots straight, a single density was used for the whole column rather than the log being integrated.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 4, 'Down to 2500 m below mudline the log transit time sits on the well''s own compaction trend at every depth, and at 2500 m the two read the same value. What does that agreement tell you about the section above 2500 m?', '["It is guaranteed by construction, because the well trend was fitted to the transit times of that same section.","It is normally compacted and in hydrostatic equilibrium, because the pore fluid there was free to escape upward as the grains packed closer.","It carries no information, because a compaction trend only becomes meaningful below the depth where overpressure begins.","It is overpressured throughout, by an amount too small for the sonic to resolve."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A section that tracks its trend is a section whose pore fluid has been free to leave, so the pressure in its pores is a connected fluid column and nothing more. Reading the agreement as circular is a classic error, because the well trend comes from the header parameters of 656 us/m, 220 us/m and 0.0006 per m rather than from a fit to the log.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 5, 'At 3000 m below mudline the log reads 297.76677602422825 us/m while the well trend says 292.070315 us/m. What does that tell you about the shale?', '["Sound is taking longer to cross a metre of that rock than the compaction law predicts, so the shale is more porous than its depth implies and is undercompacted.","The shale is more compacted than its depth implies, because a larger transit time means faster rock.","The trend was fitted to the wrong picks, because a log can never depart from a correct trend.","The density log is missing over that interval, so the transit time has been filled in by Gardner."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A larger transit time means slower rock, slower means more porous, and more porous at that depth means the shale did not compact the way the section above it did. Reading the larger transit time as faster rock is the classic sign inversion on this plot.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 6, 'Undercompacted shale is the signal the whole discipline rests on. What does it imply about the pore fluid and the pressure?', '["The fluid has drained away completely, so the pore pressure sits below the hydrostatic line.","The overburden at that depth is reduced, because the rock above is lighter than expected.","The fluid that should have been expelled during burial is still there, so it carries load the grain framework cannot take up and the pore pressure sits above hydrostatic.","The shale is denser than the trend predicts, so the grain framework carries more of the load."]'::jsonb, 2, 'If the fluid cannot leave as burial proceeds, the grains cannot pack closer and the fluid takes up the extra load, which lifts the pore pressure above hydrostatic. The high transit time and the high pressure are two readings of the same stalled event.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 7, 'A sonic log never measures pressure. What does it measure that makes it useful for pore pressure work?', '["Pore fluid density directly, which can then be turned into a fluid column.","How compacted the rock is, and in a shale that failed to dewater, compaction and pressure are locked together.","Overburden stress directly, by weighing the rock column above the tool.","The mud weight in the hole, which tracks the formation pressure while drilling."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sonic reports transit time, which is a proxy for how far compaction has progressed. The link to pressure exists only because in an undercompacted shale the two are locked together.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 8, 'You have found that the log leaves the well trend at 2500 m below mudline. What is the Beginner tier''s job with that observation?', '["Read the pore pressure straight off the frame panel at 3000 m and report it in MPa.","Run Eaton with an exponent of 3 over the log yourself and report pore pressure at every sample.","Ignore the departure until a direct pressure measurement is available to confirm it.","State the depth at which the departure begins and describe the shape of the gap, leaving the conversion into MPa to the Professional tier''s Eaton run."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At this tier you identify and describe the departure, and the frame you built is what the next tier''s prognosis stands on. The frame panel reads the frame at a depth and does not compute a pore pressure, so nothing can be read off it in MPa.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 9, 'Below 2500 m the log stands above the well trend by a widening amount: 297.76677602422825 against 292.070315 us/m at 3000 m, 282.5387777324301 against 273.391003 us/m at 3500 m, and 270.92263512383806 against 259.553028 us/m at 4000 m. What does a steadily widening gap indicate?', '["A sealed compartment, which would show as an abrupt step that then stays flat.","A washed out hole, which would show as noise rather than a systematic departure.","A pressure ramp, with excess building at a roughly constant rate per metre of depth.","A trend fitted over too little depth, which would make the gap close again below the picks."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The shape of the departure is information, not just its existence. A gap that grows steadily downward is the signature of a ramp, while an abrupt departure that stays flat would tell a different story.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 10, 'Which sanity check catches a density log that has been read in g/cc where the engine expected kg/m3?', '["The check that the overburden sits above the hydrostatic everywhere below the mudline, because a column a thousand times too light would cross below it.","The check that the fitted trend passes through the shale picks.","The check that every pressure is quoted with its depth attached.","The check that both pressure curves increase monotonically with depth."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A crossing says the sediment column is lighter than the water in it, which is not a rock, and a units error of a thousand is the usual cause. On this well the overburden leads everywhere, reaching 91.123067 MPa against 41.408580 MPa at 4000 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 11, 'The fit through the twelve shale picks returns a mudline transit time of 650.0000000000014 us/m and a compaction constant of 0.7000000000000015 per km. Which observation shows those are physically sensible?', '["They agree with the well header values of 656 us/m and 0.6 per km.","The mudline value sits close to the matrix transit time of 220 us/m.","The constant is negative, so the trend decays with depth as it should.","The mudline value sits well above the matrix transit time of 220 us/m, and the constant is positive and of order one per km."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An uncompacted near seabed mud should be far slower than the matrix, and a positive constant of order one per km is the band real clastic sections fall in. A negative constant or a mudline value below the matrix is a fit failure wearing the costume of a result.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 12, 'Which sanity check costs nothing to run and catches errors that none of the other five can see?', '["Confirming that the fitted trend passes through the shale picks.","Quoting every pressure with its depth attached, so that a value is a statement someone can check, repeat or disprove.","Confirming that the hydrostatic and the overburden are equal at the mudline.","Confirming that both pressure curves increase monotonically with depth."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pressure without a depth is not a result, because nothing about it can be verified. The same applies to a compaction constant quoted without its unit, since per m and per km differ by a factor of a thousand.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 13, 'Forgetting the seawater column on this well leaves what hydrostatic at 4000 m below mudline, and how does the error behave with depth?', '["40403398 Pa, the sediment part alone, a fixed shortfall of the 1005181.625 Pa seawater term at every depth.","40403398 Pa, with a shortfall that grows steadily as depth increases.","41408579.625 Pa, because the seawater term is already contained in the sediment integration.","1005181.625 Pa, because dropping the seawater term also removes the sediment column below it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two part column is 1005181.625 Pa of seawater plus 40403398 Pa of pore fluid, giving the graded 41408579.625 Pa. Dropping the first term removes the same amount at every depth, so it is proportionally worst in the shallow section.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 14, 'A frame is built with g taken as 9.81 m/s2 instead of the engine''s 9.80665 m/s2. What does it report for hydrostatic pressure at 4000 m below mudline, and how does that score?', '["41.422725000 MPa, which passes because the error is only about 14 kilopascals.","41.380500000 MPa, which passes because it rounds to the graded value.","41.422725000 MPa, which misses the graded 41.408579625 MPa by more than the 0.01 tolerance and is marked wrong.","41.408579625 MPa, because a change of that size does not survive to the fourth decimal place."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The whole hydrostatic column is 4222500 kg/m2, so the pressure is that mass times g and nothing else. A g of 9.81 gives 41.422725000 MPa, an error of 0.014145375 MPa against a 0.01 window, so the field fails even though the number looks right.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-frame', 15, 'The twelve shale picks return 650.0000000000014 us/m and 0.7000000000000015 per km, while the well header labels its own trend 656 us/m and 0.6 per km. What is the correct reading of that difference?', '["The fit failed to converge, because a correct fit must recover the header parameters.","The header is authoritative, so the fitted values should be discarded in favour of 656 us/m and 0.6 per km.","The matrix transit time handed to the fit was wrong, because 220 us/m applies only to the header trend.","Neither is wrong. The picks were drawn on a different trend from the one in the header, and a fit reports what the picks actually say."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A fitted trend describes the data you gave it. That is why the lithology of the picks is your responsibility, and why the fitted parameters must be checked for physical sense before a prognosis is built on them.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 1, 'In the eight step Beginner workflow, which step produces no number of its own and yet controls every number that follows?', '["Building the hydrostatic column, because it is the reference every later pressure argues against.","Stating the references: 4000 m of section below the mudline in 100 m of water, seawater at 1025 kg/m3, pore fluid at 1030 kg/m3 and gravity at 9.80665 m/s2.","Fitting the compaction trend, because the fitted parameters feed everything downstream.","Applying Gardner, because it supplies the densities the overburden integration needs."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every depth, both pressure curves and the whole integration are referenced to the values stated in step one, and no later step can detect that they were stated wrongly. The hydrostatic, the fit and Gardner all produce numbers of their own.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 2, 'A reviewer finds that the density over one interval of a submitted frame came from Gardner''s relation rather than from a density log. What should the reviewer ask about it?', '["Nothing, because Gardner returns a measured density and carries no more uncertainty than the log itself.","Whether the interval lies below the ramp top at 2500 m, since Gardner applies only to normally compacted section.","Whether the Gardner densities were converted out of kg/m3 before they entered the integration.","Which interval it covers and why the density log is absent there, since a Gardner density is an estimate standing where a measurement should be and it feeds straight into the overburden integration."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gardner supplies a density from velocity only where the log is absent, and that density feeds the overburden integration, so how much of the column was estimated is part of the frame''s provenance. Treating a Gardner density as a measurement is the classic error, because the overburden of 91.12306695073282 MPa at 4000 m below mudline is only as good as the densities under it.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 3, 'What does Gardner''s relation contribute to this workflow, and where does its output go?', '["A density from velocity in intervals where the density log is absent, which then feeds the overburden integration.","A pore pressure from velocity, which is compared against the hydrostatic line.","A transit time from depth, which is compared against the log to find the departure.","A mud weight from pressure, which bounds what the driller can pump."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Density logs stop, wash out, or were never run in the shallow section, and Gardner fills those gaps so the integration can continue. It produces a density and nothing else.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 4, 'Which course is the formal prerequisite for this one, and what does it contribute?', '["Petrophysics, which supplies the porosity that the compaction trend is fitted to.","Well Correlation, which supplies the zone boundaries the overburden is integrated between.","Well Data, which is where the sonic is despiked, washed out intervals are flagged and the depth reference is settled.","Rock Physics, which supplies the fluid densities the hydrostatic column uses."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Well Data is the prerequisite because it delivers step one of this workflow already done. Petrophysics and Well Correlation contribute later and Rock Physics sits downstream of this course.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 5, 'What does the Petrophysics course contribute to the compaction trend step of this workflow?', '["The seawater density used in the upper part of the hydrostatic column.","The matrix transit time of 220 us/m that the fit holds fixed.","The value of gravity used throughout the pressure calculations.","The shale volume that decides which points are shale and therefore eligible as picks for the fit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A normal compaction trend is a statement about shale, so the shale flag decides which points can enter the fit. A shale flag that is wrong there produces a confident trend fitted to the wrong lithology here.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 6, 'A reviewer''s first question about a submitted pressure frame is not about physics. What is it?', '["How many samples the log contains and at what spacing they were recorded.","What the depths mean, since every curve here is indexed in metres below mudline with 100 m of water above and total depth at 4000 m below mudline.","What mud weight was in the hole while the sonic was being recorded.","Which Eaton exponent was used to produce the pressure curves."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Without the depth reference on the face of the frame, a reviewer cannot tell whether a value at 3000 m is 3000 m of rock or 2900 m of rock under 100 m of sea. It is the cheapest check on the list and it catches the most expensive error.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 7, 'The hydrostatic on this well is the one number in the frame a reviewer can check entirely by hand. Which two terms make it up?', '["40403398 Pa of pore fluid alone, since the seawater is above the frame and outside it.","1005181.625 Pa of seawater alone, since the sediment section is handled by the overburden.","1005181.625 Pa of seawater to the mudline plus 40403398 Pa of pore fluid below it, giving 41408579.625 Pa.","Two sediment terms, one above and one below the overpressure ramp top at 2500 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Seawater is 1025 x 9.80665 x 100 and the pore fluid part is 1030 x 9.80665 x 4000, and the total is the graded 41.408579625 MPa at 4000 m below mudline. Dropping the seawater term is the commonest error in offshore work.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 8, 'A frame is submitted that used g = 9.8 m/s2. What hydrostatic does it report at 4000 m below mudline, and does the capstone field pass?', '["41.380500000 MPa, an error of -0.028079625 MPa against the graded value, so it fails the 0.01 window.","41.380500000 MPa, which passes because the tolerance on that field is 0.5.","41.408579625 MPa, because rounding g does not change the answer at that precision.","41.422725000 MPa, which is the value a g of 9.8 produces and it passes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The graded value is 41.408579625 MPa with a tolerance of 0.01, and a g of 9.8 lands 0.028079625 MPa below it. Both common roundings of g fail this field, which is why the constant used should be recorded with the frame.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 9, 'Why is the compaction constant the unit that reviewers get wrong most often?', '["The two forms are numerically identical, so nobody notices which one was intended.","The same constant is a thousand times smaller when written per m than per km, as the well''s own 0.0006 per m and 0.6 per km show, and both forms look like plausible numbers.","A constant written per m is a thousand times larger than the same constant written per km.","The two forms differ by a factor of a hundred, which is small enough to slip through a review."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per m and per km differ by a factor of a thousand in the direction that makes the per m form the smaller number. The capstone grades this field in per km, so a per m entry is off by three orders of magnitude.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 10, 'Converted to equivalent mud weight referenced to sea level, which pair of values brackets every pressure this well can hold at total depth?', '["1025 kg/m3 from the seawater and 1030 kg/m3 from the pore fluid.","1900 kg/m3 at the mudline and 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 at 4000 m from the density log.","1960.612149304395 kg/m3 from Gardner and 2266.333384 kg/m3 from the overburden.","1029.878049 kg/m3 from the hydrostatic and 2266.333384 kg/m3 from the overburden."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The hydrostatic at total depth gives an equivalent mud weight of 1029.878049 kg/m3, just under the 1030 kg/m3 pore fluid because the seawater part of the column is slightly lighter, and the overburden gives 2266.333384 kg/m3. Any pore pressure a later tier reports must land between those two.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 11, 'Why must fitted trend parameters be reported together with the picks they came from?', '["Without the picks a reviewer cannot tell a shale trend from a sand trend, or a well constrained fit from one squeezed into a short depth window.","Because the platform stores the picks and the parameters in the same record.","Because the picks themselves are graded fields on the capstone.","Because the parameters cannot be converted into MPa unless the picks are attached."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fitted trend without its picks is an assertion rather than a checkable result. Reported together, the trend can be checked against the picks, the mudline value against the matrix, and the constant against the per km band.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 12, 'The two pressure fields on the capstone share the tightest window on the paper. What is it?', '["A tolerance of 0.5 on hydrostatic pressure at TD and on overburden stress at TD.","A tolerance of 0.005 on hydrostatic pressure at TD and on overburden stress at TD.","A tolerance of 0.01 on hydrostatic pressure at TD and on overburden stress at TD, both graded in MPa.","A tolerance of 0.01 on hydrostatic pressure at TD and on the Gardner density at 1600 m/s."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hydrostatic at TD is graded at 41.408579625 MPa and overburden at TD at 91.12306695073282 MPa, both with a tolerance of 0.01. Both are deterministic column calculations with no interpretation in them, which is why the window is narrow.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 13, 'The capstone asks for the fitted NCT mudline transit time. What is the graded value, and what happens if you enter the well header''s figure instead?', '["The graded value is 656 us/m, and the fitted 650.0000000000014 us/m would fail instead.","The graded value is 650.0000000000014 us/m, and 656 us/m still passes because it is within 0.5.","The graded value is 220 us/m, which is the matrix value the fit holds fixed.","The graded value is 650.0000000000014 us/m, and the header''s 656 us/m misses it by more than the 0.5 tolerance, so the field fails."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The field asks specifically for the value the least squares fit recovers from the twelve picks. Entering the header trend value where the fitted one is graded is the classic loss on this paper, because the two are different numbers and the gap exceeds the tolerance.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 14, 'The capstone grades the fitted compaction constant in a field whose unit label is 1/km. What should you enter, and what is the tolerance?', '["0.0006, the per m form used for the well''s own trend, with a tolerance of 0.005.","0.7000000000000015, the per km form, with a tolerance of 0.005.","Either form, because the grader accepts per m and per km for this field.","0.7000000000000015, the per km form, with a tolerance of 0.5."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field is graded per km with a tolerance of 0.005, so an entry near 0.7 passes. A per m entry is a thousand times smaller and scores nothing, which is why the unit label should be read before typing into the field.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-pore-pressure-workflow', 15, 'The Professional tier runs an Eaton prognosis with n = 3 over the same sonic. What overpressure does it recover at total depth?', '["41.408579625 MPa, which is the hydrostatic at total depth.","91.12306695073282 MPa, which is the overburden at total depth.","Exactly 6 MPa, the 4 kPa per m ramp acting over the 1500 m below 2500 m.","Whatever value the Beginner frame panel reports for pore pressure at total depth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sonic below 2500 m was built by encoding the ramp into the transit times, so the prognosis recovers the imposed pressures exactly. The frame panel reads the frame at a depth and does not compute a pore pressure at all.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A colleague reports the hydrostatic pressure at 4000 m below mudline in the golden well as 40.403398 MPa. What did they do, and what does it do to any pore pressure estimate built on that curve?', '["They omitted the seawater column, worth 1005181.625 Pa, so the baseline is low and pore pressure estimates come out too high","They used 9.81 m/s2 for gravity, so the baseline is high and pore pressure estimates come out too low","They used the depth below sea level in the sediment term, so the baseline is high","They swapped the seawater and pore fluid densities, which leaves the total unchanged"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The graded value is 41.408579625 MPa and the shortfall is exactly the seawater term. A baseline that is too low exaggerates every departure measured against it.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Which depths belong in the two terms of the hydrostatic column at total depth in the golden well?', '["The sediment term uses 4000 m and the seawater term uses 4100 m","The sediment term uses 4000 m below mudline and the seawater term uses the 100 m water depth, and neither term uses 4100 m","Both terms use 4100 m, the depth below sea level","The sediment term uses 4100 m and the seawater term uses 100 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Total depth is 4000 m below mudline, which is 4100 m below sea level, and putting 4100 m into the sediment term counts the water twice, once as seawater and once as pore fluid.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Which curves does the Associate tier build, and which are added later?', '["This tier builds hydrostatic and fracture pressure, and the Professional tier adds pore pressure and overburden","This tier builds all four curves and the Professional tier recomputes them","This tier builds hydrostatic and overburden, and the Professional tier adds pore pressure from the departure of the log from the trend and then fracture pressure","This tier builds pore pressure and fracture pressure, and the Professional tier adds hydrostatic and overburden"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two computed curves come first because an estimate of pore pressure is a comparison, and a comparison against a bad reference produces a confident number describing nothing. Fracture pressure needs a pore pressure curve before it can be built.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'The hydrostatic equivalent mud weight at total depth is 1029.878049 kg/m3 and the overburden equivalent mud weight is 2266.333384 kg/m3. What do those two values bound?', '["The range of mud weights that can be drilled without setting any casing","The uncertainty on the hydrostatic calculation at total depth","The range of pore fluid densities present in the well","Every pressure the well can hold at total depth, with the pore pressure inside the bracket and the mud weight above the pore pressure and below the fracture pressure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Converting the frame into density units puts the whole well design into one unit that the drilling side already works in. The pore pressure and the fracture pressure both sit inside the bracket.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'An equivalent mud weight computed for the hydrostatic column at total depth comes out at exactly 1030 kg/m3. What is the most likely cause?', '["The seawater column was left out of both the pressure and the height, so the calculation collapsed to the pore fluid density","Gravity was rounded to 9.81 m/s2","The bulk density log was left in g/cc","The compaction trend was fitted to picks in us/ft"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With the seawater removed from both the numerator and the denominator, the expression reduces to the pore fluid density by construction. The correct value is 1029.878049 kg/m3, marginally below 1030 kg/m3 because the top 100 m of the column is lighter seawater.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Two engineers compute the hydrostatic pressure at 4000 m below mudline in the golden well and get 41.408579625 MPa and 41.380500000 MPa. What most likely differs between them?', '["The second used the depth below sea level in the sediment term","The second used g = 9.8 m/s2, which is low by 0.028079625 MPa and outside the 0.01 MPa tolerance","The second used g = 9.81 m/s2, which is high by 0.014145375 MPa","The second omitted the seawater column, worth 1005181.625 Pa"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rounding gravity to 9.8 m/s2 gives exactly 41.380500000 MPa on this column. Omitting the seawater would have given 40.403398 MPa instead, which is a much larger and differently signed error.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'What happens to a shale during burial when its pore fluid cannot escape?', '["The seawater column above the mudline raises the pore pressure without affecting compaction","The rock compacts normally and the departure from the trend appears only where the density log is missing","The fluid carries part of the increasing load, its pressure rises above hydrostatic, and compaction stalls, leaving the rock more porous and slower than its burial depth suggests","The grain frame takes the whole load, so the rock compacts faster and becomes denser than its burial depth suggests"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Load during burial is shared between the grain contacts and the pore fluid, and trapped fluid relieves the grains of part of it. That retained porosity is the physical trace a sonic log can see.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'At 2500 m below mudline the log reads 317.2847498247154 us/m and the well trend predicts 317.2847498247154 us/m, while at 3000 m the log reads 297.76677602422825 us/m against a trend value of 292.070315 us/m. What do those two comparisons show together?', '["The log is above the trend at both depths, so the whole section is overpressured","The trend must be wrong, because a log should never cross its compaction trend","The log is faster than the trend at 3000 m, so the section below the ramp is normally compacted","The log sits on the trend at the top of the ramp and is slower than the trend below it, which is the undercompaction signature"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A transit time above the trend means the rock is slower, and therefore more porous, than a normally compacted rock at that depth. The agreement at 2500 m is what fixes the top of the ramp.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'If the shallow bulk density log were replaced by a Gardner estimate, which of the six graded capstone numbers would move?', '["The overburden stress at total depth, 91.12306695073282 MPa, because it is an integral of density over depth","The hydrostatic pressure at total depth, 41.408579625 MPa","The fitted compaction constant, 0.7000000000000015 per km","The trend transit time at 2500 m, 317.2847498247154 us/m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the overburden consumes the density log. The hydrostatic column needs only water depth, the two fluid densities, gravity and depth, and the trend values depend on transit times rather than on density.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Gardner''s relation returns 1960.612149304395 kg/m3 at 1600 m/s and 2294.256693926084 kg/m3 at 3000 m/s. What does that tell you about the relation?', '["Gardner returns a pressure rather than a density, so it feeds the prognosis directly","Density changes slowly with velocity because of the one quarter exponent, which is why a rough velocity input is still usable","Density is proportional to velocity, so a ten percent error in velocity gives a ten percent error in density","Gardner can replace the hydrostatic column where no density log exists"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nearly doubling the velocity moves the density by a few hundred kg/m3, which is the effect of the exponent of one quarter. That insensitivity is both the weakness of the relation and the reason it survives a rough velocity.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Why does the hydrostatic column have to be built before any pore pressure estimate is attempted?', '["The sonic log cannot be read until the hydrostatic curve exists","Hydrostatic pressure is measured directly and therefore takes precedence over the other curves","A pore pressure estimate is a statement about a departure from hydrostatic, so an error in the baseline moves every estimate built on it","The hydrostatic column is a required input to the least squares fit of the compaction trend"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The baseline is the reference the departure is measured against, and a constant error in it shifts every derived value by the same amount. Hydrostatic is computed rather than measured.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Which quantity in the frame is independent of the geology between two wells at the same depth and water depth?', '["The overburden stress, since integrating a density log removes local variation","The pore pressure, since it is referenced to a standard trend","The fracture pressure, since it is added at a higher tier","The hydrostatic pressure, since it needs only the water depth, the fluid densities, gravity and the depth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two wells with the same water depth and the same formation water density share a hydrostatic curve whatever lies between them. The overburden depends on the actual rock, so two wells a kilometre apart can differ measurably at the same depth.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Lost circulation lowers the fluid level in the wellbore. Why can that cause a kick elsewhere in the open hole?', '["The bottom hole pressure falls with the level, and if it drops below the pore pressure of another exposed bed that formation flows into the well","The mud lost into one bed raises the pore pressure of that bed until it flows","The fracture pressure of the weakest exposed bed rises once mud has been lost","The overburden stress falls once mud has left the wellbore"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two failure modes are connected through the height of the mud column, which is why the window has to be respected at every depth in the open hole at once. Overburden is set by the overlying section and does not respond to the mud.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'A bulk density curve arrives in g/cc, reading 1.900 at the mudline, and is fed to an overburden integration that expects kg/m3. What happens, and what is the correct value in kg/m3?', '["The hydrostatic pressure comes out a thousand times too small, and the correct value is 1900 kg/m3","The overburden comes out a thousand times too small, and the correct value is 1900 kg/m3","The overburden comes out a thousand times too large, and the correct value is 1900 kg/m3","Nothing changes, because the integration normalises the units, and the correct value is 1.900 kg/m3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The factor between g/cc and kg/m3 is a thousand, and only the overburden consumes the density curve. The bulk density at the mudline in the golden well is 1900 kg/m3.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Which statement describes the two frame curves of this well correctly from the mudline to TD?', '["They are equal at the mudline at 1.005182 MPa and diverge below it, reaching 91.123067 MPa and 41.408580 MPa at TD","They are parallel, separated by a constant amount all the way from the mudline to TD","They are equal at the mudline and stay within a few tenths of a MPa of each other down to TD","The hydrostatic is above the overburden in the shallow section and below it at depth"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Above the mudline both curves are the same seawater column, and below it the overburden accumulates bulk density while the hydrostatic accumulates a fixed 1030 kg/m3 pore fluid, so the gap widens with depth. Assuming the curves are parallel is a classic error and it hides the widening gap that overpressure occupies.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'A learner reports the hydrostatic pressure at TD as 41.422725000 MPa. What happened, and how is it marked?', '["They forgot the seawater column, and the answer is still inside tolerance","They used g = 9.81 m/s2 instead of 9.80665, so the answer is high by 0.014145375 MPa and fails the 0.01 MPa tolerance","They used g = 9.8 m/s2, so the answer is low by 0.028079625 MPa","They used the pore fluid density of 1030 kg/m3 for the whole column, water depth included"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The graded value is 41.408579625 MPa, computed with g = 9.80665 m/s2. This is the awkward rounding error because 9.81 produces an answer that looks right and is out by about 14 kilopascals, which the capstone marks wrong.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Gardner fills a 500 m gap in the density log and returns densities that are slightly light. Where does that error appear?', '["Only within the 500 m interval that was filled","Only in the pore pressure, since the overburden does not depend on the density log","In the overburden at every depth below the gap, and so in the effective stress at those depths, because the integration carries a running sum","In the hydrostatic, which is built from the same density log"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in pore pressure work is local, because every quantity is an accumulated column and an error above your depth of interest arrives there intact. The hydrostatic in option 4 is built from fixed fluid densities and never reads the density log.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'The well trend and the fitted trend share a matrix transit time of 220 us/m. At the mudline they read 656.000000 us/m and 650.000000 us/m, and at TD they read 259.553028 us/m and 246.148327 us/m. Which parameter is responsible for the separation growing with depth?', '["The matrix transit time, because it sets the floor that both curves approach","The mudline transit time, because that is where the two curves already differ","Neither parameter, because the separation between two trends is the same at every depth","The compaction constant, because it sets the rate of decay, so its effect compounds with depth while the influence of the mudline value is scaled down by the exponential"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two trends carry 0.0006 per m, which is 0.6 per km, against a fitted 0.7000000000000015 per km, and a difference in rate opens the gap steadily with depth. Option 1 cannot separate them because the matrix value of 220 us/m is fixed at the same figure for both, and option 3 is the classic assumption that two trends run parallel.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Which two quantities of this well can be built without any compaction model at all?', '["The hydrostatic and the overburden, because they need only densities, depths and gravity","The overburden and the normal compaction trend","The normal compaction trend and the fitted trend","The hydrostatic and the twelve shale picks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is why the frame is the reliable part of the work and the first pair of curves to check when a prognosis looks strange. Both trends in options 2 and 3 depend on parameters that were either labelled or fitted, so neither is independent of a compaction model.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'At 3000 m the overburden is 66.831143 MPa, the hydrostatic is 31.307730 MPa, the log reads 297.76677602422825 us/m and the well trend reads 292.070315 us/m. Which conclusion is supported at this tier?', '["The pore pressure at 3000 m is 66.831143 MPa","The pore pressure at 3000 m lies above 31.307730 MPa and below 66.831143 MPa, because the log sits above the trend","The pore pressure at 3000 m equals the hydrostatic, because the log and the trend are close together","The pore pressure at 3000 m cannot be bounded at all without more information"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The frame supplies the floor and the ceiling, and the departure of the log from the trend says the answer is above the floor. Option 1 would mean zero effective stress and a parted formation, and turning the departure into a number in MPa is work for the tier above this one.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Why does the choice between the header trend and the fitted trend matter for a well you have already logged?', '["Because the two trends disagree only below 2500 m","Because changing the trend changes the overburden as well","Because the trend is the reference the log is compared against at every depth, so two trends that differ, for example 259.553028 us/m against 246.148327 us/m at TD, give two different answers from one sonic log","Because the header trend is quoted to fewer significant figures"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two curves differ at every depth below the mudline, starting at 656.000000 us/m against 650.000000 us/m at the mudline itself. The overburden in option 2 comes from densities and depths and is untouched by the trend.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'What does a least squares fit through shale picks actually guarantee?', '["That the fitted curve is the normal compaction trend of the well","That the picks used were taken in shale","That the fitted parameters are physically sensible","Only that the fitted curve is the best description of the points it was given"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A fitted trend describes the data you gave it, so hand it the wrong points and it returns a confident number describing the wrong points. The first three are checks you have to make yourself, because nothing in the fit output distinguishes good picks from bad ones.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Which single check would have exposed the disagreement between the fitted trend and the well header?', '["Plotting both curves on the sonic log and seeing which one the log follows above 2500 m","Recomputing the least squares fit and carrying more decimal places","Confirming that the residuals of the fit are small","Confirming that all twelve picks lie above the matrix transit time of 220 us/m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The residuals here are at machine precision and prove only that the picks are consistent with each other, so option 3 catches nothing. The matrix check in option 4 is a guard the engine already enforces before the fit runs.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'A fitted trend for a marine clastic well comes back with a mudline transit time of 300 us/m. What is the right response?', '["Accept it, because the fit minimised the residuals","Distrust it, because sediment at the seabed is water rich and should read in the high hundreds of us/m, as the 656 us/m of this well does","Accept it, because it is safely above the matrix value of 220 us/m","Refit with the matrix value left free so that the mudline value can rise"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Checking that a fitted parameter describes a rock is a judgement the fit cannot make for you. Being above the matrix value is necessary and not sufficient, and freeing the matrix value makes the fit ill conditioned because the floor and the decay rate trade off against each other.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Four of the twelve picks sit at 2600, 2900, 3200 and 3500 m, below the 2500 m top of the overpressure ramp. How should that observation be treated?', '["It proves that the fitted trend is wrong","It is irrelevant, because the fit returned zero residuals","It is a check worth running, because picks taken below the onset of overpressure are a standard way to bias a normal trend, so the picks should be shown to be normally pressured shale","It means the matrix transit time should be raised above 220 us/m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In this well all twelve picks lie exactly on one exponential, so the disagreement with the header is that the picks were drawn on a different trend, and the pick depths are not what caused it. The depth check is still the habit to keep, because in a real well that pattern of picks is a common source of a biased trend.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'At 2500 m the well trend reads 317.284750 us/m and the fitted trend reads 294.722796 us/m. A report quotes 317.284750 us/m as the fitted value. Which error is that?', '["A unit error between per m and per km","Forgetting the seawater column in the frame","Treating Gardner''s transform as exact","Reading the header trend as the fitted trend, which are two different curves built from two different parameter pairs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fit returned 650.0000000000014 us/m and 0.7000000000000015 per km, while the header carries 656 us/m and 0.0006 per m, so the two curves separate immediately below the mudline. Quoting one where the other is meant is the commonest confusion in this module.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Which statement about Gardner''s transform is correct for an interval logged with sonic only?', '["You have no choice but to lean on it, and you should record which samples came from it, because it is an empirical fit rather than a law","The interval should be left out of the overburden integration, since estimated density is worse than none","It is exact for clastics, so no record of its use is needed","It should be refitted from the well''s own density log every time, which removes its empirical character"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Leaving the interval out shifts every overburden value below it, so option 2 makes the problem worse. The engine marks each density sample as coming from the log or from Gardner, so a later reader can see which parts of the overburden rest on a measurement and which rest on an estimate.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'An overburden curve for this well reads 0 MPa at the mudline. What is wrong with it?', '["Nothing, the overburden is zero at the mudline by definition","The seawater column was left out, so the curve is light by 1.005182 MPa at the mudline and at every depth below it","The first density sample was extended upward to the mudline","The trapezoidal integration used too coarse a sample spacing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The integration is seeded with 1025 x 9.80665 x 100 = 1005181.625 Pa before it touches the first density sample. Forgetting the water column is the commonest error in the whole calculation and it is silent, because the profile still looks plausible while being about a megapascal light everywhere.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Which statement is true of the golden well''s frame at every depth below the mudline?', '["The log transit time lies above the well trend, with the gap widening steadily downward.","The hydrostatic gains more pressure per thousand metres than the overburden does.","The fitted trend lies above the well trend, since it starts higher at the mudline.","The overburden exceeds the hydrostatic, reaching 91.12306695073282 MPa against 41.408579625 MPa at 4000 m below mudline."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Below the mudline the rock is denser than the fluid in it, so the overburden must lead everywhere. The log lies above the well trend only below 2500 m, and the fitted trend lies below the well trend at every depth in the table, starting at 650.000000 against 656.000000 us/m at the mudline.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'A submitted frame reports the compaction trend at 2500 m below mudline as 294.722796 us/m. What has been used?', '["The log transit time, which reads the same value at that depth.","The fitted trend, when the well''s own trend at that depth reads 317.2847498247154 us/m.","The matrix transit time, which the fit holds fixed at 220 us/m.","The fitted mudline transit time, which the fit recovers as 650.0000000000014 us/m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 2500 m the well trend and the log agree at 317.2847498247154 us/m while the fitted trend reads 294.722796 us/m. The capstone grades the well trend for that field, so reporting the fitted trend there is a lost mark rather than a physics error.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'Two frames differ only in the value of gravity used. One reports 41.422725000 MPa at total depth and the other 41.380500000 MPa. What can you conclude?', '["The first forgot the seawater column and the second did not.","The difference between them is inside the tolerance, so both would be marked correct.","The first used 9.81 m/s2 and the second used 9.8 m/s2, and both fail the 0.01 window against the graded 41.408579625 MPa.","One used a pore fluid density of 1025 kg/m3 and the other used 1030 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The whole hydrostatic column is 4222500 kg/m2, so the pressure is that mass times g. The errors are +0.014145375 and -0.028079625 MPa against a 0.01 window, so both rounded values of g are marked wrong.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Why does the departure at 2500 m show on the sonic track and not on the pressure track?', '["The hydrostatic and the overburden are built from fluid columns and densities, so they run smoothly through 2500 m, and only the comparison of log against trend picks the change out.","The pressure track is plotted at too coarse a scale to resolve a change of that size.","The overburden kinks at 2500 m but the hydrostatic curve plots over it and hides the kink.","The overpressure ramp starts below the deepest pressure sample in the frame."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither pressure curve knows anything about the compaction state of the shale, so neither flickers at the ramp top. The departure exists only in the difference between what the log reads and what the trend predicts.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Which error corrupts the overburden integration and every depth label at once while still leaving both pressure curves monotonic?', '["Using the wrong coefficient in Gardner''s relation for the shallow section.","Using a depth reference of below rotary table or below sea level as though it were below mudline.","Using an Eaton exponent that has not been calibrated to the well.","Using a matrix transit time other than 220 us/m in the trend fit."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On this well below sea level and below mudline differ by the 100 m of water, so a mixed reference shifts every sample and starts the rock in the wrong place. The curves still rise smoothly, which is why the monotonic check cannot catch it and the mudline equality check can.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'A reviewer is handed fitted trend parameters of 650.0000000000014 us/m and 0.7000000000000015 per km with no picks attached. What can the reviewer not determine?', '["Whether the compaction constant is positive.","Whether the mudline value exceeds the matrix transit time of 220 us/m.","Whether the trend decays with depth rather than growing.","Whether the picks were shale or some other lithology, and whether they spanned enough depth to constrain both parameters at once."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sign, decay direction and the comparison against the matrix can all be read from the parameters alone. Lithology and depth span can only be read from the picks, and the twelve picks on this well span 200 m to 3500 m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'A candidate enters 656 for the fitted NCT mudline transit time and 0.0006 for the fitted compaction constant. How do those two fields score?', '["Both fail. 656 misses 650.0000000000014 us/m by more than the 0.5 tolerance, and 0.0006 misses 0.7000000000000015 per km by far more than the 0.005 tolerance.","Both pass, since each is the value the well header quotes for its own trend.","Only the transit time fails, since the compaction constant is accepted in either unit.","Only the compaction constant fails, since 656 us/m is within the 0.5 tolerance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'These are the two classic losses on this paper: entering the header trend value where the fitted one is graded, and entering the compaction constant in per m where the field is labelled 1/km. Neither is a near miss.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which of the six graded capstone fields is read from a velocity rather than from a depth, and what is its graded value and tolerance?', '["NCT transit time at 2500 m, 317.2847498247154 us/m, tolerance 0.5.","Fitted NCT mudline transit time, 650.0000000000014 us/m, tolerance 0.5.","Gardner density at 1600 m/s, 1960.612149304395 kg/m3, tolerance 0.5.","Overburden stress at TD, 91.12306695073282 MPa, tolerance 0.01."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Gardner field takes a sonic velocity of 1600 m/s as its input and the depth setting does not enter it. The other three fields named are all read at a depth, at 2500 m, at the mudline and at total depth respectively.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Why is the hydrostatic the one value in this frame that can be checked entirely by hand?', '["Because the density log over this well is smooth enough to average by eye.","Because it is the only field graded to a tolerance of 0.01.","Because Gardner supplies its densities directly from the sonic velocity.","It is two constant density columns added together, 1025 x 9.80665 x 100 and 1030 x 9.80665 x 4000, giving 41408579.625 Pa at 4000 m below mudline, with no rock property in it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The hydrostatic depends only on two fluid densities, a water depth and a section length, so nothing has to be integrated. The overburden cannot be checked the same way because it integrates a density that changes from 1900 kg/m3 at the mudline to 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 at total depth.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'A frame passes every sanity check and is handed on with no statement of where the log leaves the trend. What is wrong with it?', '["Nothing, because the numbers are the deliverable and the reading belongs to the prognosis.","It is half a deliverable, because the departure depth and the shape of the gap are the reading the next tier actually needs.","It fails the monotonic check, because an unstated departure implies a dip in one of the curves.","It cannot be assessed at all until an Eaton run at n = 3 is attached to it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On this well the log sits on the trend down to 2500 m, where both read 317.2847498247154 us/m, and stands above it below that with the gap widening. Stating that depth and that shape is the last thing the Beginner tier does.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'A later tier reports a pore pressure at total depth whose equivalent mud weight exceeds 2266.333384 kg/m3. What does that tell you?', '["It is acceptable, because an overpressured well can carry a pore pressure above its own overburden.","It is expected, because equivalent mud weight is referenced to sea level rather than to the mudline.","Something upstream is wrong, because the hydrostatic at 1029.878049 kg/m3 and the overburden at 2266.333384 kg/m3 bracket every pressure this well can hold.","It means the pore fluid density was taken as 1025 kg/m3 rather than 1030 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those two equivalent mud weights are the physical bounds on the well, so a reported pore pressure above the upper one indicts either the prognosis or the frame under it. This is why the bracket is worth computing at Beginner even though the pressure it bounds is computed later.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Two capstone fields concern the compaction trend. Which trend does each of them use?', '["The fitted mudline field grades the fit''s own 650.0000000000014 us/m, while the NCT field at 2500 m uses the well''s own trend and grades 317.2847498247154 us/m.","Both fields use the fitted trend, since the capstone asks for a least squares result throughout.","Both fields use the well''s own trend of 656 us/m, 220 us/m and 0.0006 per m.","The mudline field uses the well''s own trend and the 2500 m field uses the fitted trend."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two trends sit next to each other and are easy to swap, which is why the capstone names which one each field wants. At the mudline the fitted trend reads 650.000000 us/m and the well trend reads 656.000000 us/m.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The twelve shale picks on this well span 200 m to 3500 m, and that span is what lets the fit pin both parameters at once. What goes wrong if every pick comes from a 300 m window instead?', '["The fit refuses to converge and returns no parameters at all.","The curvature that separates the mudline value from the compaction constant is not present in the data, so the fit trades one against the other and the extrapolation above and below the picks is unreliable.","The mudline value is forced to the header figure of 656 us/m.","The compaction constant comes back negative, so the trend grows with depth."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An exponential needs leverage across depth to constrain both of its parameters. A fit over a short window still runs through the picks and still returns confident numbers, which is why the extrapolated part of such a trend should be reported as an assumption.'),
  ('porepressure', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'The density column on this well runs from 1900 kg/m3 at the mudline to 2505.265301734371 kg/m3 at 4000 m. What does an overburden that plots as a straight line against that column tell a reviewer?', '["The integration was performed correctly, since a rising density produces a straight pressure curve.","The seawater term was dropped, since the missing 1005181.625 Pa is what puts the bend in the curve.","The depth reference is below sea level rather than below mudline.","A single density was used for the whole column instead of the log being integrated, and that should be declared rather than implied by the plot."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A density that grows with depth must produce an overburden that steepens with depth, so a straight overburden contradicts the density column beside it. On this well the curve bends, going from 1.005182 MPa at the mudline to 91.123067 MPa at 4000 m.');
